                    <li>Set vendor criteria and picked Austin-aligned partners like SnapKitchen, CookUnity and La Colombe</li>
                    <li>Projected $8K to $12K net revenue per station a year, with 30% reinvested into UT Dining</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        @* ===================== Starbucks ===================== *@
        <div class="mp-proj mk-sbux" id="mk-proj-starbucks" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mk-title-starbucks" hidden>
            <div class="mk-deck" role="group" aria-roledescription="slideshow" aria-label="Starbucks slides">
                <div class="mk-stage">

                    <div class="mk-slide sb-cover" data-title="Hook">
                        <video class="sb-bg" src="/videos/projects/starbucks-cover.mp4" poster="/videos/projects/starbucks-cover.jpg" muted loop playsinline autoplay aria-label="Four weeks of one person’s orders fill a calendar: an oat latte every weekday, a Frappuccino and cake pop on Saturday, a hazelnut latte on Sunday. A gold scan finds the three patterns and fills in next week by itself"></video>
                        <p class="sb-kick">Starbucks app strategy</p>
                        <h4 class="sb-title">Starbucks’ app holds $12 billion a year. <em>It’s a glorified gift card.</em></h4>
                        <p class="sb-lede"><b>35.8M</b> people open it to pay. Almost nobody opens it to plan: regulars tap through the same order every morning, and <b>2 in 3</b> orders still aren’t placed ahead in the app. Here’s how to make it a daily ritual instead.</p>
                        <ul class="sb-chips">
                            <li><b>35.8M</b>active Rewards members</li>
                            <li><b>33%</b>of orders placed in the app</li>
                            <li><b>59%</b>of store spend from members</li>
                        </ul>
                        <span class="sb-src">Source: Starbucks Card, Loyalty &amp; Mobile dashboard, Q3 FY26 (U.S.)</span>
                        <span class="sb-sig" aria-hidden="true">suhani ✦</span>
                    </div>

                    <div class="mk-slide" data-title="Summary" hidden>
                        @Html.Raw(Top(sbuxSections, 0, "Starbucks: App &amp; loyalty strategy"))
                        <h4 class="mk-h">Starbucks can grow by turning its app from a checkout tool into <em>a daily ritual</em>.</h4>
                        <ol class="mk-scr">
                            <li><span>the situation</span><p>Starbucks takes <b>48%</b> of U.S. coffee-shop spending, and <b>35.8M</b> active Rewards members already drive <b>59%</b> of U.S. store spend.</p></li>
                            <li><span>the catch</span><p>Yet <b>67%</b> of transactions still aren’t mobile orders, a share that has moved just <b>4 points</b> in three years. People use the app to pay, not to discover or plan.</p></li>
                            <li><span>the fix</span><p>Build habit-forming features first (customization profiles, smart filtering, recurring orders), manage to one metric, <b>Weekly Active Ritual Orders per User</b>, and capture up to <b>$1.3B</b> a year.</p></li>
                        </ol>
                        @Html.Raw(Foot("Technomic (2025); Starbucks Card, Loyalty &amp; Mobile dashboard, Q3 FY26; author analysis", 2, 9))
                    </div>

                    <div class="mk-slide" data-title="The gap" hidden>
                        @Html.Raw(Top(sbuxSections, 1, "Starbucks: App &amp; loyalty strategy"))
                        <h4 class="mk-h">Starbucks takes nearly half of U.S. coffee-shop spending, but only <em>1 in 3</em> orders is placed ahead in the app.</h4>
                        <div class="mk-split">
                            <figure class="mk-exhibit">
                                <figcaption>Exhibit 1: Every 100 U.S. orders, by how they’re placed</figcaption>
                                <div class="sb-waffle" role="img" aria-label="33 of every 100 U.S. orders are mobile orders; 67 are placed some other way">
                                    @for (var i = 0; i < 100; i++)
                                    {
                                        <i class="@(i < 33 ? "on" : "")" style="--n: @i"></i>
                                    }
                                </div>
                                <p class="sb-legend"><span class="on"><b>33</b> ordered ahead in the app</span><span><b>67</b> everywhere else: register, drive-thru, delivery</span></p>
                            </figure>
                            <div class="mk-side">
                                <p class="mk-exlabel">The app today</p>
                                <ul class="mk-kpis">
                                    <li><b>48%</b>of U.S. coffee-shop spending goes to Starbucks</li>
                                    <li><b>59%</b>of U.S. store spend comes from Rewards members</li>
                                    <li><b>$3.1B</b>loaded onto Starbucks cards in a single quarter</li>
                                </ul>
                                <p class="mk-sowhat"><b>Key takeaway</b>The growth isn’t new customers. It’s moving visits Starbucks already has into the app.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        @Html.Raw(Foot("Technomic (2025); Starbucks Card, Loyalty &amp; Mobile dashboard, Q3 FY26", 3, 9))
                    </div>

                    <div class="mk-slide" data-title="Plateau" hidden>
                        @Html.Raw(Top(sbuxSections, 1, "Starbucks: App &amp; loyalty strategy"))
                        <h4 class="mk-h">Members keep growing. <em>The habit doesn’t.</em></h4>
                        <div class="sb-plateau">
                            <figure class="mk-exhibit">
                                <figcaption>Exhibit 2: Mobile orders, % of U.S. transactions, by quarter</figcaption>
                                <ol class="sb-cols" aria-label="Mobile orders held between 29% and 33% of transactions for twelve straight quarters">
                                    @for (var i = 0; i < sbMop.Length; i++)
                                    {
                                        <li class="@(i >= 9 ? "up" : "")" style="--h: @Pct(sbMop[i] * 100.0 / 40)%; --n: @i"><b>@sbMop[i]</b><i></i><span>@sbQ[i]</span></li>
                                    }
                                </ol>
                                <p class="sb-flat"><span>8 straight quarters at 31%</span></p>
                            </figure>
                            <div class="sb-tiles">
                                <div class="sb-tile">
                                    <p class="mk-exlabel">Active Rewards members</p>
                                    <b>35.8M</b>
                                    <span><em>+10%</em> since Q4 FY23</span>
                                    <svg viewBox="0 0 100 30" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="@Spark(sbMembers)" /></svg>
                                </div>
                                <div class="sb-tile">
                                    <p class="mk-exlabel">Dollars loaded on cards</p>
                                    <b>$12.3B</b>
                                    <span>in the last four quarters, peaking each holiday</span>
                                    <svg viewBox="0 0 100 30" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="@Spark(sbLoaded)" /></svg>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <p class="mk-sowhat inline"><b>Key takeaway</b>Starbucks keeps winning the wallet: members grew 10% while the share of orders placed ahead rose just 4 points.</p>
                        @Html.Raw(Foot("Starbucks Card, Loyalty &amp; Mobile dashboards (Oct 2025, Jul 2026), U.S. company-operated stores", 4, 9))
                    </div>

                    <div class="mk-slide" data-title="Prize" hidden>
                        @Html.Raw(Top(sbuxSections, 1, "Starbucks: App &amp; loyalty strategy"))
                        <h4 class="mk-h">One extra ritual order a week from 1 in 10 members is worth <em>$1.3B a year</em>.</h4>
                        <div class="sb-prize">
                            <ol class="sb-eq" aria-label="Base case">
                                <li><b>35.8M</b>active Rewards members</li>
                                <li><i>×</i><b>10%</b>who set up a ritual</li>
                                <li><i>×</i><b>1</b>extra order a week</li>
                                <li><i>×</i><b>52</b>weeks</li>
                                <li><i>×</i><b>~$7</b>average ticket*</li>
                                <li class="sum"><i>=</i><b>$1.30B</b>added revenue a year</li>
                            </ol>
                            <figure class="mk-exhibit">
                                <figcaption>Exhibit 3: Added revenue a year, $B, by adoption and extra ritual orders a week</figcaption>
                                <table class="sb-heat">
                                    <thead><tr><th scope="col"><span class="visually-hidden">Members who adopt</span></th>@foreach (var o in perWeek) { <th scope="col">@Pct(o) a week</th> }</tr></thead>
