                                <ul><li>Lifestyle-based recurring orders</li><li>Predictive order suggestions</li><li>Habit-loop rewards</li></ul>
                                <p class="mk-test"><b>Test</b>“Schedule this weekly?” prompt after checkout</p>
                            </div>
                            <div class="mk-branch">
                                <h5><i>B</i>Health-aware customization</h5>
                                <p class="mk-why"><b>Problem</b>Health preferences aren’t saved, and healthy choices get no guidance.</p>
                                <ul><li>Saved preference profiles</li><li>Guided healthy customization</li><li>Goal-based ordering</li></ul>
                                <p class="mk-test"><b>Test</b>Auto-applied modifiers vs. manual customizing</p>
                            </div>
                            <div class="mk-branch">
                                <h5><i>C</i>Personalized recs &amp; deals</h5>
                                <p class="mk-why"><b>Problem</b>Offers ignore behavior, and the full menu overwhelms discovery.</p>
                                <ul><li>Context-aware upsells</li><li>AI behavioral deal engine</li><li>Guided discovery</li></ul>
                                <p class="mk-test"><b>Test</b>Personalized offer vs. a generic coupon</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        @Html.Raw(Foot("Opportunity solution tree, author analysis", 6, 9))
                    </div>

                    <div class="mk-slide" data-title="Priorities" hidden>
                        @Html.Raw(Top(sbuxSections, 3, "Starbucks: App &amp; loyalty strategy"))
                        <h4 class="mk-h">Habit features score highest, so <em>ship the quick wins first</em>, fund the bets and cut paid line-skipping.</h4>
                        <figure class="mk-exhibit grow">
                            <figcaption>Exhibit 4: RICE score by feature (Reach × Impact × Confidence ÷ Effort), colored by impact/effort quadrant</figcaption>
                            <p class="sb-quads"><span class="win">Quick win: high impact, low effort</span><span class="bet">Strategic bet: high impact, high effort</span><span class="fill">Fill-in</span><span class="cut">Deprioritize</span></p>
                            <ol class="mk-bars sb-rice">
                                @for (var i = 0; i < rice.Length; i++)
                                {
                                    var (feature, score, quad) = rice[i];
                                    <li class="@quad" style="--w: @Pct(score * 100.0 / riceMax)%; --n: @i">
                                        <span class="mk-bar-label">@feature</span>
                                        <span class="mk-bar"><i></i></span>
                                        <span class="mk-bar-val">@score.ToString("N0")</span>
                                    </li>
                                }
                            </ol>
                        </figure>
                        <p class="mk-sowhat inline"><b>Key takeaway</b>Two of the top three are quick wins, and all three make the next order faster than the last. Every one is judged by WARO.</p>
                        @Html.Raw(Foot("RICE scoring and impact/effort matrix, author analysis", 7, 9))
